WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.497
The dashboard is a high-value system because it concentrates security data and may control integrations.

00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:13.630
Protect it with individual accounts, strong passwords, and multi-factor authentication where available.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:17.947
Remove default credentials and avoid shared administrator accounts.

00:00:17.947 --> 00:00:25.443
Patch the operating system, dashboard software, databases, libraries, and supporting services through a managed process.

00:00:25.443 --> 00:00:31.123
Test significant updates, back up configuration, and retain a rollback path.

00:00:31.123 --> 00:00:37.256
Unsupported components create long-term risk even when the interface appears to work.

00:00:37.256 --> 00:00:44.298
Place the dashboard on an appropriate network segment and restrict inbound and outbound communication.

00:00:44.298 --> 00:00:47.706
Collectors should reach only required services.

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:53.385
Administrative interfaces should not be exposed directly to the public internet.

00:00:53.385 --> 00:01:00.428
Use a controlled VPN, authenticated gateway, or similarly protected method for legitimate remote access.

00:01:00.428 --> 00:01:11.559
Store API keys, database passwords, signing keys, and service credentials in protected secret storage rather than source files, browser code, or shared notes.

00:01:11.559 --> 00:01:19.964
Give each integration narrow permissions, rotate credentials when needed, and revoke them when a service is retired.

00:01:19.964 --> 00:01:24.281
Use encrypted connections where appropriate and validate certificates.

00:01:24.281 --> 00:01:31.323
Apply secure session settings, rate limits, input validation, and defenses supplied by maintained frameworks.

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:40.637
Backups and exported reports deserve the same protection as the live dashboard because they may contain equivalent sensitive data.

00:01:40.637 --> 00:01:46.316
Log administrative sign-ins, privilege changes, integration updates, exports, and retention changes.

00:01:46.316 --> 00:01:50.633
Review unusual activity and protect the audit trail.

00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:59.492
Security controls should support authorized operations without enabling covert access, bypassing controls, or hiding actions from accountable oversight.
